<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1689">1689</a>–<a href="/years/1752">1752</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Abdul Latif Bhittai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and the most celebrated poet of the Sindhi language. His collected verse, the Shah Jo Risalo, retells local folk romances as allegories of the soul's search for God. He lived under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhora-dynasty">Kalhora dynasty</a>, and his shrine at Bhit Shah remains a major place of pilgrimage and music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-abdul-latif-bhittai.jpg" alt="Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fitrat69 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shah_Abdul_Latif_Bhitai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bhittai was born in Hala Haweli, near modern Hala, to Shah Habib, a great-grandson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poet Shah Abdul Karim Bulri, in a Sayyid family whose ancestors had come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in the late 14th century. The family moved to nearby Kotri Mughal. Local tradition holds that he was illiterate, but his use of Arabic and Persian and the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> show that he was well educated. Most of what is told of his life was passed down orally and written down in the late 19th century by Mirza Qalich Beg and Mir Abd al-Husayn Sangi.</p><p>At about twenty he left home and travelled for three years through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and beyond, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thatta">Thatta</a>, Kalachi (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>), Hinglaj in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Balochistan</a> with a band of Jogis, Lasbela, Dwarka, Junagadh, Nagarparkar and Jaisalmer. After the killing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghun</a> nobleman Mirza Mughal Beg in 1713 he married his daughter Saida Begum, who died a few years later; he did not remarry and had no children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His verses were not written down in his lifetime but sung and memorised by his disciples at his musical sessions, and compiled after his death as the Shah Jo Risalo. The German philologist Ernest Trumpp first published it in 1866. It has thirty chapters, each called a Sur and tied to a musical mode; the most widely accepted version holds some 3,000 abyat and 200 wa'is. Some Surs retell Sassui Punhun, Sohni Mehar, Umar Marui and Lilan Chanesar; Sur Kedaro laments <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a>. He kept the Qur'an and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Mathnawi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> with him, and his poems sometimes translate Rumi's verses. Though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> passed from Mughal rule to the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhora-dynasty">Kalhora dynasty</a> in his lifetime, his poetry never mentions politics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His following reportedly earned him the hostility of nobles and of Noor Mohammad Kalhoro, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, who is said to have tried to poison him before becoming his disciple. Some ten years before his death he settled on a sandhill near Hala Haweli, later called Bhit Shah. He died there on 21 December 1752, and Mian Ghulam Shah Kalhoro built a tomb over his grave in 1754 or 1765. His poetry is sung there every Thursday evening, and his three-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Urs</a> falls on 14 Safar. Sindhi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> revere him too: he visited the Hinglaj Mata temple, and his Sur Ramkali was composed in reverence to Hinglaj Mata and the jogis who came there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The great Sindhi Sufi poet <b>Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai</b> lived under their rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalhora-dynasty">Kalhora dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hala, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in Bhit, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.81,68.43&amp;year=1729" aria-label="Hala on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 5334h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 5334h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.81,68.43&amp;year=1729">What was here in 1729? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
